To implement Brent's cycle-detection algorithm using a single pointer and return the starting node of the cycle, we can use the following approach:

1. Initialize a slow pointer at the head of the linked list and a fast pointer at the head as well.
2. Move the slow pointer one node at a time and the fast pointer two nodes at a time.
3. If the fast pointer reaches the end of the linked list, break the loop and return null (no cycle exists).
4. If the fast pointer reaches a node that has been visited before (i.e., it enters a cycle), then the slow pointer will be at a node that is a power of two distance behind the fast pointer in the cycle.
5. To find the starting node of the cycle, move the slow pointer to the head of the linked list and move both pointers at the same pace (one node at a time) until they meet again. The node where they meet is the starting node of the cycle.

Here's the implementation in Java:
```java
public ListNode detectCycle(ListNode head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) {
        return null;
    }

    ListNode slow = head;
    ListNode fast = head;

    // Find meeting point
    do {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;
    } while (slow != fast);

    // Move slow pointer to head and move both pointers at the same pace
    slow = head;
    while (slow != fast) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next;
    }

    return slow;
}
```
This implementation has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1). It can detect a cycle and return the starting node of the cycle in a single traversal after detecting a cycle.

Note that this implementation assumes that the linked list has at least one node. If the linked list can be empty, you should add a check for that before the loop.

Also, this implementation can handle linked lists with multiple cycles and return the starting node of the first cycle encountered while traversing the linked list. This is because once the fast pointer enters a cycle, it will eventually meet the slow pointer again, regardless of how many cycles there are or where they are located. Once the meeting point is found, the slow pointer is moved back to the head of the linked list, and both pointers move at the same pace until they meet again, which will be at the starting node of the first cycle encountered.

