def remove\_overlapping\_dates(df, X):
df['date'] = pd.to\_datetime(df['date'])
df = df.sort\_values('date')

removed\_dates = set()
for i in range(df.shape[0]):
current\_date = df.loc[i, 'date']
if current\_date in removed\_dates:
continue

for j in range(i+1, df.shape[0]):
if (current\_date - df.loc[j, 'date']).days <= X \* 7:
removed\_dates.add(df.loc[j, 'date'])
else:
break

return df[~df['date'].isin(removed\_dates)]

result = remove\_overlapping\_dates(df, X)

END SOLUTION

The solution involves sorting the dataframe by date and then iterating through the rows. For each row, it checks if the date is already in the set of removed dates. If it is, it skips to the next row. If not, it adds the date to the set of removed dates and then checks the next X weeks to see if any of those dates are already in the dataframe. If they are, it adds them to the set of removed dates. It continues this process until it reaches a date that is more than X weeks away from the current date. At that point, it starts checking the next row. The function returns a new dataframe that contains only the rows that do not overlap with any other rows.

